---
// PILLAR — Athletic Management / "Momager".
//
// ENTRANCE — TREATMENT C, image-first. The section opens on the photograph
// before any type at all; the kicker and lede land below it and to the right,
// offset from the photo's left edge rather than aligned to it. Everyday
// Legends above opens on a hairline head and the chapter break below is the
// pivot, so nothing adjacent is matched.
//
// COLUMN WINDOW — A, the page default: columns 3-8.
//
// RHYTHM — open. The largest step on the page other than the chapter break's
// own, which spends the open step twice over.
//
// OVERLAP 1 OF 3 — the copy block climbs INTO the Cashmere band and crosses
// its bottom edge, so the photograph's ground and the section's type occupy
// the same rectangle instead of stacking. Contrast is safe on both sides of
// the boundary by construction: Obsidian on Cashmere is 10.9:1 and Obsidian
// on Porcelain is 15.6:1, so a line that straddles the edge is legible along
// its whole length. Negative margin, not position:absolute — it survives any
// resize.
//
// THE PLATE'S WIDTH — FIXED HERE. Phase D made .plate-panel a two-track grid:
// the photograph in columns 1-6, plus a second track of flat Cashmere
// deliberately RESERVED as legible ground for RunningHead, which was fixed to
// the right trim at 9.5px and unreadable over the picture. Phase E.5 deleted
// RunningHead.astro. The reservation survived it and was still live on this
// branch — a 708px photograph beside 732px of empty ground with a hard seam
// down the middle of the page. The band is now a single width ending at the
// COLUMN-8 EDGE, which is the body measure's right edge, so the photograph
// and the copy below it terminate on the same line.
//
// THE BLEACHERS FRAME IS OUT. CONTEXT.md records mcclain-bleachers.jpg as
// retired — it is the one candid in a set of composed, camera-facing frames,
// glancing off-axis and seated loosely, and the standing rule is that a
// photograph whose register fights the page does not earn a smaller
// placement, it comes out. It was still rendering here as a second inset. Its
// removal also settles the image budget: one inset on the page, not three.
//
// Crop provenance for the frame that stays. From brand_assets/mcclain-field.jpg
// (no EXIF orientation flag):
//   -primary  extract left 816, top 900, 1140 x 1425  (4:5, torso-up; the
//             window sits high so white bleachers, not turf, fill the ground
//             behind her)
// Verified with sharp().metadata(), never sips.
//
// COPY — ⚠️ THIS SECTION IS SHORT BY TWO PARAGRAPHS. COPY.md Part 1 approves
// three body paragraphs here and only the first is present on this branch;
// Phase E.6 is recorded as shipped in CONTEXT.md but is not in this repo's
// history. E.8 is a composition pass under a zero-copy-changes constraint, so
// it is NOT restoring them — that is a copy pass and it needs its own branch.
// Flagged in the E.8 report.
//
// NO HAND-SET grid-column ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import fieldPrimary from '../assets/mcclain-field-primary.jpg';
---

<section class="athletics" id="sec-athletics">
  <!-- The entrance: the photograph, before any type. Full-width Cashmere
       ground so the ground reads as a ground; the photograph inside it meets
       the left trim only and stops at the column-8 edge. -->
  <div class="plate-panel">
    <figure class="plate-figure plate-img">
      <Image
        src={fieldPrimary}
        alt="Dr. Syreeta McClain on the field in a black blazer, cropped close"
        widths={[480, 720, 960, 1140]}
        sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 96vw, 66vw"
        quality={80}
        loading="eager"
        decoding="sync"
      />
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="spread athletics-copy">
    <!-- No rule and no numeral above the type: the picture was the entrance.
         Kicker outdented to column 2, lede on the body edge at column 3,
         paragraphs indented to column 4 — the three edges. -->
    <div class="athletics-head col-kicker row-1">
      <h2 class="kicker">Athletic Management</h2>
      <p class="role">Momager | Athletic Manager to the McClain Brothers</p>
    </div>

    <p class="lede col-body row-2">Long before NIL became part of college athletics, there were early mornings, long drives, practices, recruiting conversations, academic decisions, sacrifices, victories, disappointments, and countless lessons learned along the way.</p>

    <div class="body-copy col-indent row-3">
      <p>As the mother of three football players navigating the high school and Division&nbsp;I levels, Dr.&nbsp;McClain&rsquo;s role as &ldquo;Momager&rdquo; grew organically from motherhood into strategy.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Live margin, split across two rows on purpose. The numeral sits on
         ROW 2 beside the lede: the lede here runs ten lines and the body
         runs three, so with both in row 3 the margin was empty for the whole
         height of the display type and the caption ended up a long way from
         the photograph it describes. The caption stays on ROW 3 beside the
         body, which is the only place its baseline can be snapped to a body
         baseline — display type is exempt from the lattice, so a caption
         hung beside the lede cannot be. Both are last in source order for
         their rows, so at 1280 they fall inline after the copy. -->
    <span class="athletics-numeral section-numeral section-numeral--head col-numeral row-2" aria-hidden="true">06</span>

    <p class="plate-caption athletics-caption col-margin row-3">On the field, direct gaze</p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* RHYTHM: open. */
  .athletics{ margin-top: var(--vr-open); }

  /* --- The Cashmere band, carrying the section's entrance --------- */
  .plate-panel{
    background-color: var(--cashmere);
    /* Cashmere is a ground here, not a wash — but it still layers rather
       than sitting flat. */
    background-image:
      radial-gradient(96% 62% at 12% 8%,  rgba(231,226,221,0.34) 0%, rgba(231,226,221,0) 62%),
      radial-gradient(70% 58% at 88% 92%, rgba(71,68,64,0.14) 0%, rgba(71,68,64,0) 60%);
    display: grid;
    /* ONE track for the picture, ending at the COLUMN-8 EDGE — the same line
       the body measure below it ends on. The old second track was a grid
       reservation left behind by a deleted component; see the header note.
       Expressed against the page grid's own custom properties, so it tracks
       the trim at every width rather than being pinned to a pixel value. */
    grid-template-columns:
      calc(var(--grid-left) + var(--grid-pad) + 8 * var(--col-w) + 7 * var(--grid-gap))
      minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: center;
    /* Bottom padding is deep on purpose: the copy block below climbs back up
       into it, and this is the room that overlap needs. */
    padding: clamp(88px, 11vw, 160px) 0 clamp(120px, 14vw, 210px);
  }

  .plate-figure{
    grid-column: 1;
    /* 4:5 — used nowhere else now that the 4:5 seated portrait has become a
       2:3 inset. The four photographs on the page run 3:4, 2:3, 4:5 and 5:7. */
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
  }

  .plate-figure :global(img){
    object-position: 50% 38%;
    /* The running track behind her is the one out-of-palette area left after
       the crop. Measured on the rendered frame: this grade lands the track at
       ~24deg hue and under 0.18 saturation — muted clay, inside the warm
       earth family, without the sepia cast a hue-mapping filter would give
       it. Sepia was tested and rejected: no other photograph on the site uses
       one, and it broke tonal consistency with the leopard portrait. */
    filter: grayscale(0.58) contrast(1.05) saturate(0.42);
  }

  /* Weighted to the BOTTOM of the frame, where the track and turf sit.
     Multiplying Gold over them carries both to a warm neutral. Every stop is
     a locked palette value. */
  .plate-figure::before{
    background: linear-gradient(to top,
      var(--gold)      0%,
      var(--gold)     14%,
      var(--cashmere) 38%,
      var(--porcelain) 66%,
      var(--porcelain) 100%);
    opacity: 0.52;
  }

  .plate-figure::after{
    background:
      linear-gradient(to top,  rgba(26,22,19,0.28) 0%, rgba(26,22,19,0) 42%),
      linear-gradient(270deg,  rgba(209,199,189,0.36) 0%, rgba(209,199,189,0) 24%);
  }

  /* --- OVERLAP 1 OF 3 ---------------------------------------------
     The copy climbs into the band and crosses its bottom edge. z-index so it
     paints over the Cashmere rather than under it; position:relative only to
     make z-index apply — no offsets are set here, so nothing is pinned. */
  .athletics-copy{
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    margin-top: calc(-1 * clamp(64px, 8vw, 128px));
  }

  .athletics-head{ margin-bottom: var(--s5); }

  /* --- Live margin ------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Hung at the outer trim, like the funnel index numerals below. The tier's
     own -0.045em tracking already lands the last glyph short of its box, so
     no negative margin is added on top of it — stacking one put the numeral
     past the trim. Measured. */
  .athletics-numeral{
    align-self: start;
    text-align: right;
    /* Optically against the lede beside it, not on the lattice: display type
       is exempt, and a 66px glyph snapped to a 28px grid reads as snapped. */
    margin-top: clamp(48px, 6vw, 96px);
  }

  .athletics-caption{
    align-self: start;
    text-align: right;
    /* Six baselines down, so its top edge cannot line up with the paragraph
       beside it — that alignment is what makes a margin read as a second
       column instead of as a margin — and a whole number of baselines, so
       its own baseline lands on a body baseline. */
    margin-top: calc(6 * var(--baseline));
    margin-left: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 1279px){
    /* Inline in the measure: both go flush left again. */
    .athletics-numeral,
    .athletics-caption{ text-align: left; }
    .athletics-numeral{ margin-top: var(--vr-tight); }
    .athletics-caption{ margin-top: var(--baseline); }
  }

  @media (max-width: 1024px){
    /* The band keeps its one-sided bleed: the photograph runs off the left
       trim, a strip of Cashmere on the right. */
    .plate-panel{
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) var(--gutter);
      padding: clamp(64px, 12vw, 104px) 0 clamp(88px, 15vw, 132px);
    }
    .plate-figure{ aspect-ratio: 4 / 5; }

    /* The overlap holds at phone width too — it is expressed in vw and needs
       no separate value — but it is scaled back, because at 390px a deep
       climb would put the kicker on top of the picture rather than on the
       ground below it. */
    .athletics-copy{ margin-top: calc(-1 * clamp(40px, 9vw, 64px)); }
  }
</style>
